Section 3 — Adult Health and Aged Care
oe ie
Www
Acute myocardial infarction.
Polymyositits.
Acute glomerulonephritis.
Rhabdomyolysis.
Carcinoma of the bladder.
MOOR >
MCQ 3.161
A 55-year-old woman presented with an
episode of intense cramping abdominal
pain, resolving after one hour. Abdominal
examination was non-contributory. Urinalysis
showed a trace of blood in the urine.
Plain abdominal X-ray and abdominal
computerised tomography (CT) with
intravenous contrast were done after the
pain had subsided; these investigations
showed no evidence of urinary calculus. The
transverse cut of the CT, in its arterial phase
at the level of the second lumbar vertebra,
is illustrated. Which one of the following is
the most likely diagnosis?
Annular pancreas.
Polycystic kidney disease.
. Acute glomerulonephritis.
Horseshoe kidney.
Urate calculus.
MOQO2>
MCQ 3.162
A 20-year-old man from Egypt is in Australia visiting relatives. He presents with a history
of urinary problems over the past two weeks, with frequency, dysuria, fever and
terminal haematuria. He has also noted occasional haemospermia. On examination
he appears a little pale, with a temperature of 37.8°C. A urine specimen is positive for
blood and protein. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','{"A":"Urinary amoebiasis.","B":"Urinary bilharziasis (schistosomiasis).\nUrinary calculus.","C":"Urinary tuberculosis.","D":"Urinary leptospirosis.","E":"moO"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[293],"source_qid":"3.161","duplicate_group_id":"exact-e136022733f9","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"c70675953217d1aaa0ac","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":293,"source_qid":"3.161"}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"c70675953217d1aaa0ac","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":293,"supporting_pages":[293],"source_qid":"3.161","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"Australian Medical Council\nSection 3 — Adult Health and Aged Care\noe ie\nWww\nAcute myocardial infarction.\nPolymyositits.\nAcute glomerulonephritis.\nRhabdomyolysis.\nCarcinoma of the bladder.\nMOOR >\nMCQ 3.161\nA 55-year-old woman presented with an\nepisode of intense cramping abdominal\npain, resolving after one hour. Abdominal\nexamination was non-contributory. Urinalysis\nshowed a trace of blood in the urine.\nPlain abdominal X-ray and abdominal\ncomputerised tomography (CT) with\nintravenous contrast were done after the\npain had subsided; these investigations\nshowed no evidence of urinary calculus. The\ntransverse cut of the CT, in its arterial phase\nat the level of the second lumbar verte