~ Section 3 = Adult Health and Aged Care
. Dilutional hyponatraemia.
. Pseudohyponatraemia.
. Ectopic antidiuretic hormone (ADH).
. Diabetes insipidus.
. Renal salt wasting.
MCQ 3.172
A 63-year-old man with ischaemic heart disease and congestive cardiac failure is
admitted because of fatigue and worsening shortness of breath.
He is on medications of frusemide, aldactone, digoxin, potassium supplements and
metoprolol.
On examination his tongue is dry and firm. He has no oedema. The jugular venous
pulse (JVP) is not seen while supine and his lung bases are clear. He is in atrial fibrillation
and his BP is 120/80mmHg supine.
His serum biochemistry is shown below:
Sodium *126mmol/L (135-145)
Potassium *3.2mmol/L —_(3.8-4.9)
Chloride 96mmol/L (95-110)
Urea *26mmol/L (3.0-8.0)
Creatinine *0.18mmol/L (0.06-0.12)
Glucose *17,.0mmol/L (3.0-7.7)
Which one of the following is the most likely reason for his low sodium level?
Ectopic antidiuretic hormone (ADH).
Excessive diuresis.
Dilutional hypo-osmolar hyponatraemia.
Non-hypo-osmolar pseudohyponatraemia.
Renal salt wasting.
mOOR>
MCQ 3.173
Achest X-ray of a 70-year-old woman presenting with a chronic cough shows a 4x3cm
opacity associated with the right main bronchus. She also has symptoms of malaise
and loss of energy with weight loss, polyuria, and constipation. Bronchoscopic biopsy
is reported as a squamous cell carcinoma of the lung. Her serum calcium is found to
be 3.3mmol/L (1.8-2.6). Which one of the following is the most likely explanation for
the hypercalcaemia?','{"A":"Production of parathyroid hormone-related peptide (PTHrP).\nPrimary hyperparathyroidism.","B":". Renal failure with phosphate retention.","C":"Bone metastases.","D":"Dehydration.","E":"MOQP>"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[301],"source_qid":"3.172","duplicate_group_id":"exact-d0007d985f16","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"acd867788f17b9446800","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":301,"source_qid":"3.172"}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"acd867788f17b9446800","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":301,"supporting_pages":[301],"source_qid":"3.172","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"Australian Medical Council\n~ Section 3 = Adult Health and Aged Care\n. Dilutional hyponatraemia.\n. Pseudohyponatraemia.\n. Ectopic antidiuretic hormone (ADH).\n. Diabetes insipidus.\n. Renal salt wasting.\nMCQ 3.172\nA 63-year-old man with ischaemic heart disease and congestive cardiac failure is\nadmitted because of fatigue and worsening shortness of breath.\nHe is on medications of frusemide, aldactone, digoxin, potassium supplements and\nmetoprolol.\nOn examination his tongue is dry and firm. He has no oedema. The jugular venous\npulse (JVP) is not seen while supine and his lung bases are clear. He is in atrial fibrillation\nand his BP is 120/80mmHg supine.\nHis serum biochemistry is shown below:\nSodium *126mmol/L (135-145)\nPotassium *3.2mmol/L —_(3.8-4.9)\nChloride 96mmol/L (95-110)\nUrea *26mmol/L (3.0-8.0)\nCreatinine *0.18mmol/L (0.06-0.12)\nGlucose *17,.0mmol/L (3.0-7.7)\nWhich one of the following is the most likely reason for his low sodium level?\nEctopic antidiuretic hormone (ADH).\nExcessive diuresis.\nDilutional hypo-osmolar hyponatraemia.\nNon-hypo-osmolar pseudohyponatraemia.\nRenal salt wasting.\nmOOR>\nMCQ 3.173\nAchest X-ray of a 70-year-old woman presenting with a chronic cough shows a 4x3cm\nopacity associated with the right main bronchus. She also has symptoms of malaise\nand loss of energy with weight loss, polyuria, and constipation. Bronchoscopic biopsy\nis reported as a squamous cell carcinoma of the lung. Her serum calcium is found to\nbe 3.3mmol/L (1.8-2.6). Which one of the following is the most likely explanation for\nthe hypercalcaemia?","options":{"A":"Production of parathyroid hormone-related peptide (PTHrP).\nPrimary hyperparathyroidism.","B":". Renal failure with phosphate retention.","C":"Bone metastases.","D":"Dehydration.","E":"MOQP>"},"source_answer":null,"source_explanation":null,"source_answer_status":"pending_import","current_answer":null,"current_answer_status":"needs_verification","current_evidence":[],"currentness_risk":"medium","currentness_keywords":["age","diabetes"],"likely_visual_question":true,"parser_confidence":"medium","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract","parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","stem_hash":"d0007d985f16c01bcbcc597c8d583a788bc6800d","duplicate_group_id":"exact-d0007d985f16","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"acd867788f17b9446800","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":301,"source_qid":"3.172"}]}',0,'v2.9.4','2026-09-24T00:00:00.000Z') ON CONFLICT(record_id) DO UPDATE SET source_f