Section 3- Adult Health and Aged Care | |
MCQ 3.196
In order to evaluate a new test for hepatitis C, a cohort of 1000 subjects is investigated.
This test result was positive in 38 of the 1000 subjects. Hepatitis C infection defined by
conventional testing was confirmed to be present in 20 of the 1000 patients.
Of the 38 patients with a positive result from the new test, 18 were shown to have
hepatitis C by conventional testing and 20 were false positives who did not have the
disease.
The new test was negative in 962 of the 1000 subjects; 2 of these 962 were false
negatives who were shown subsequently to have hepatitis C by conventional testing;
960 did not and were truly negative.
Results are outlined in tabular form, showing numbers of subjects in each category.
Which one of the following statements regarding this study is correct in regard to
diagnosis of hepatitis C and interpretation of test results?
Hepatitis C present Hepatitis C absent TOTAL
New test positive 18 (a) 20 = (b) 38 (a+b)
New testnegative 2 (c) 960 (d) 962 (ctd)
TOTAL 20 (atc) 980 (b+d) 1000
(atb+c+d)','{"A":"The prevalence of hepatitis C in the cohort is 20%.","B":"The sensitivity of the new test is 90%.","C":"The specificity of the new test is 85%.","D":"The positive predictive value of the new test is 65%.","E":"The negative predictive value of the new test is 90%.\n\nA 23-year-old Greek woman who is known to have mild and nonsymptomatic\nanaemia associated with beta-thalassaemia trait, presents for counselling regarding\ninheritance risk for her children. She is about to marry a 25-year-old Greek man, who\nhas no symptoms, but has recently been tested and noted also to have the same\nbeta-thalassaemia trait. Which one of the following is the probability that they will\nhave an unaffected offspring without either beta-thalassaemia or beta-thalassaemia\ntrait?\n\n0.1.\n0.25.\n. 0.5.\n0.75.\n120;\n\nTTMOOFP>"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"3.196","duplicate_group_id":"exact-8278747bdc36","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"7e7428689356645161aa","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":317,"source_qid":"3.196"}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"tesseract","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"7e7428689356645161aa","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":317,"source_qid":"3.196","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"b elite =1@ eo O\nSection 3- Adult Health and Aged Care | |\nMCQ 3.196\nIn order to evaluate a new test for hepatitis C, a cohort of 1000 subjects is 