| Section 3 — Adult Health and Aged Care
MCQ 3.213
A 45-year-old woman, who has three teenage children, has been taking the combined
oestrogen/progestogen contraceptive pill for 10 years, during which time she had
regular light menstrual periods. She ceased this medication three months ago. Apart
from the period she had immediately after ceasing it, since that time she has not
menstruated. She is extremely anxious and fears that she is pregnant, although there
are no other symptoms to suggest this diagnosis. Vaginal examination reveals a bulky
retroverted uterus. Which one of the following would be the most appropriate advice?','{"A":"Reassurance that pregnancy is rare at her age.","B":"A pregnancy test should be performed.","C":"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) level should be assessed.","D":"Resume taking the oral contraceptive pill (OCP).","E":"Review in four weeks time if she still has not menstruated.\n| pore alata Ee ee sel\n} MCQ 3.214\n\nYou are reviewing a couple who are both in their late twenties and have been trying\nto conceive for the last three years. She has had previous fallopian tube surgery\nafter tubal obstruction had been diagnosed by laparoscopy, has amenorrhoea and\ngalactorrhoea, and had evidence of mild endometriosis involving the uterosacral\nligaments treated at the time of the tubal surgery. He has a severe and persistent\nabnormality of the semen specimen. Which one of the following is the most appro-\npriate management?\n\nA. She should be treated with the dopamine agonist bromocriptine.\nB. She should be treated with danazol.\n\nC. He should be treated with gonadotrophin injections.\n\nD. She should have further laparoscopic surgery.\n\nE. They should use intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) in IVF.\n\nMCQ 3.215\n\nA 24-year-old woman presents with abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding after six\nweeks of amenorrhoea. Which one of the following combinations of physical signs is\nmost likely if she has a tubal ectopic pregnancy?\n\n. Board-like abdominal rigidity with both rebound tenderness and guarding.\nLittle guarding but marked rebound tenderness in the suprapubic region.\n\n. Profound shock with a rapid pulse and low BP.\n\n. Rapid pulse and upper abdominal rebound tenderness.\nTenderness in the pouch of Douglas and a tender adnexal mass.\n\nM oq &. >\n\nMCQ 3.216\n\nhistory of two episodes of postcoital bleeding. She has never had an abnormal Pap\nsmear and her last one was done about one year ago. Which one of the following is\nthe most likely cause of the postcoit