MCQ 3.240
| A 5-year-old boy is being reviewed with his fourth episode of nephrotic syndrome. His
} previous episodes had responded well to oral prednisolone. Despite high dose steroid
therapy (2mg/kg/day) on this occasion, his signs are slow to resolve. On examination
he is noted to be hypertensive and his urine shows microscopic haematuria. Which
one of the following investigations would most likely delineate the cause for this
presentation?
; A. Renal ultrasound.
B. Renal biopsy.
C. Isotope nuclear scan.
D. Serum complement (C3).
; E. Selective proteinuria assay.
ee SS
MCQ 3.241
A 7-day-old female infant is brought to see you because of yellow discolouration of
; the skin. She was born at term by a vacuum delivery, Apgar scores were 9 at one
minute and 10 at five minutes, birth weight 3250gm. She had an uneventful hospital
course, is fully breast-fed and was first noticed to be jaundiced on day four after she
was discharged home with a weight of 3020gm. She is passing normal stools. On
examination she is moderately jaundiced, weight is 3110gm, liver edge is palpable
2cm below the right costal margin. Otherwise examination is normal. Details of tests
are outlined below.
Mother’s blood group O Rhesus negative
Baby''s blood group B Rhesus negative.
Coombs test negative
Full blood count normal
Serum bilirubin *240umol/L (<200) with conjugated
bilirubin of 12umol/L)
