| would have predisposed to this condition?','{"A":"He was born at 36 weeks gestation.","B":"He walked early at nine months of age.","C":"His presentation throughout pregnancy and delivery was breech.","D":"He had an unexplained high fever for three days at six months.","E":"He had an undisplaced fracture of his left femur at age 14 months.\nial lilir-o<everamaeaescaiaeaneaeeaanaieniaanieieemnaiemaee\n4 MCQ 3.260\n: A 2-year-old child is seen in general practice with a two-day history of low grade\nfever, irritability and mouth ulcers. Which one of the following would be the most |\n; appropriate to establish the diagnosis?\nli A. Blood culture.\n| B. Full blood examination.\nC. Cerebrospinal fluid examination.\nD. Examine his fingers and feet for rash.\nE. Urine examination for protein and blood.\ni MCQ 3.261\n| A 6-week-old infant is seen because of irritability and difficulty feeding, wanting to\nfeed every hour and a half. He is breast fed but gaining only 60gm per week. He\nhas not had any vomiting and his bowels open every second day and are curdy\n| greenish-yellow. On examination he is alert and appears hungry but looks scrawny.\nThe remainder of his examination is normal. Which one of the following would be the\n| most appropriate next step in management?\nA. Test his stools for reducing substances.\nB. Supplement his feeds with soy formula.\nC. Suggest that his mother abandon breast feeding.\nD. Supplement his feeds with cow milk based formula.\n| E. Arrange for a series of test feeds in a parent and baby unit.\ni\n324"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"3.259","duplicate_group_id":"exact-6b4a70341cc5","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"243bf63c03322ae39a9b","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_