MCQ 3.278
An 18-month-old boy presents to the Emergency Department with a history from
his mother of one day of fever, iritability and refusal to walk. On examination he
appears miserable, his temperature is 38.5°C and he resists passive flexion of his left
leg at the hip. The remainder of his examination is normal. Hip ultrasound shows a joint
effusion and antibiotics have been commenced. Which one of the following is the most
appropriate next step in management?
Open lavage.
Pavlik harness.
. Traction.
Early mobilisation.
Pelvic spica.
MOOQORP>
MCQ 3.279
An 18-month-old infant presents with a 36-hour history from her parents of a spiking
fever, tachypnoea with grunting respirations and slight cough. On examination
her temperature is 39.5°C. She looks ill and is pale with shallow respirations. Chest
| auscultation is normal. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?
. Acute bronchitis.
Bronchiolitis.
. Lobar pneumonia.
Asthma.
Foreign body inhalation.
MOORP>
