MCQ 3.284 |
A é6-month-old boy born to Greek parents at 35 weeks gestation has been well except
for jaundice from the third to ninth day of life. He is noted to be pale and a full blood
examination shows:
Haemoglobin *90g/L (95-140)
Mean cell volume (MCV) 72$L (70-85)
Mean cell haemoglobin 35pg (32-36)
concentration (MCHC)
White cell count (WCC) 8.0x10°/L (4.0-11.0)
Lymphocytes 4.8x107/L (2-13)
Neutrophils 2.8x10?/L (1-8)
Monocytes 0.4x10°/L (0.2-1.2)
Reticulocyte count 1% (0.2-2.0)
The blood film shows anisocytosis, microcytosis and hypochromia of the erythrocytes.
Which one of the following tests would be most likely to establish the diagnosis?','{"A":"Serum ferritin.","B":"Haemoglobin electrophoresis.","C":"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (Gé6PD).","D":"Osmotic fragility of erythrocytes.","E":"Serum folic acid.\n\n—e_—_—————————\n\nMCQ 3.285\n\nA 6-year-old girl presents with a one-week history of puffy face, ankle oedema and\nabdominal distension. There are no signs of cardiac failure. Which one of the following\nis the most appropriate next step in assessment?\n\nA. Examine retina for hypertensive changes.\n\nB. Assess peripheral arterial circulation.\n\nC. Measure child''s weight and height to determine body mass index (BMI).\nD. Measure BP and urinalysis.\n\nE. Examine abdomen to assess renal size.\n\n——\n\nMCQ 3.286\n\nThe parents of a 3-year-old girl are concerned as they have noted that her urine has\nbeen a dark red-brown colour and that her urine output has decreased over the\nlast two days. Which one of the following would be the most significant additional\nhistory?"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"3.284","duplicate_group_id":"exact-c693bd967633","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"2cbbb721502d2bfe4eca","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":350,"source_qid":"3.284"}],"parser_version":"2.8-residual-resolver-selective-ocr-visual-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v28_selective_ocr_base_parser","text_method":"v28_selective_ocr","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,'text',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','low','{"record_id":"2cbbb721502d2bfe4eca","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":350,"source_qid":"3.284","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"Australian Medical Council\n0 ee eensennssssesessseessnestsnsunssnsseneeeee——s}\nMCQ 3.284 |\nA é6-month-old boy born to Greek parents at 35 weeks gestation has been well except\nfor jaundice from the third to ninth day of life. He is noted to be pale and a full blood\nexamination shows:\nHaemoglobin *90g/L (95-140)\nMean cell volume (MCV) 72$L (70-85)\nMean cell haemoglobin 35pg (32-36)\nconcentration (MCHC)\nWhite cell count (WCC) 8.0x10°/L (4.0-11.0)\nLymphocytes 4.8x107/L (2-13)\nNeutrophils 2.8x10?/L (1-8)\nMonocytes 0.4x10°/L (0.2-1.2)\nReticulocyte count 1% (0.2-2.0)\nThe blood film shows anisocytosis, microcytosis and hypochromia of the erythrocytes.\nWhich one of the following tests would be most likely to establish the diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Serum ferritin.","B":"Haemoglobin electrophoresis.","C":"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (Gé6PD).","D":"Osmotic fragility of erythrocytes.","E":"Serum folic acid.\n\n—e_—_—————————\n\nMCQ 3.285\n\nA 6-year-old girl presents with a one-week history of puffy face, ankle oedema and\nabdominal distension. There are no signs of cardiac failure. Which one of the following\nis the most appropriate next step in assessment?\n\nA. Examine retina for hypertensive changes.\n\nB. Assess peripheral arterial circulation.\n\nC. Measure child''s weight and height to determine body mass index (BMI).\nD. Measure BP and urinalysis.\n\nE. Examine abdomen to assess renal size.\n\n——\n\nMCQ 3.286\n\nThe parents of a 3-year-old girl are concerned as they have noted that her urine has\nbeen a dark red-brown colour and that her urine output has decreased over the\nlast two days. Which one of the following would be the most significant additional\nhistory?"},"source_answer":null,"source_explanation":null,"source_answer_status":"pending_import","current_answer":null,"current_answer_status":"needs_verification","current_evidence":[],"currentness_risk":"low","currentnes