Section 3 — Maternal Health
= A. Any effect of heparin on the baby can be rapidly reversed by protamine
sulphate.
; B. Rapid metabolism of heparin in the mother minimises placental transfer to the
> fetus.
\t C. Heparin crosses the placenta more slowly than do the coumarin derivatives.
pl D. The fetus cannot metabolise the coumarin derivatives, so their effect on the fetus
= is increased.
E. Reversal of the anticoagulant effect of heparin in the mother can be achieved
more quickly than that of coumarin, should labour occur.
ee
MCQ 3.309
A 19-year-old primigravida at 41 weeks of gestation has had irregular painful
contractions for the last nine hours. Pelvic examination shows the cervix is fully effaced
5 but only 2-3cm dilated. The fetal head is at the level of the ischial spines in a left
occipito-posterior (LOP) position. The membranes ruptured one hour ago. Which one
ig of the following would be the most appropriate next step in management?
of A. Oxytocic (Syntocinon®) infusion.
h B. Hydration drip of 5% dextrose.
C. Cross-match blood.
D. Caesarean section.
E. Lumbar epidural block.
> ee ee)
MCQ 3.310
A 26-year-old woman has developed a fever and malaise at 10 weeks of gestation
in her first pregnancy. Two weeks previously she had come into contact with a child
* ultimately diagnosed to have had rubella. Which one of the following would be the
